WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.027
MCP is useful when an AI application needs current information or controlled access to external capabilities.

00:00:06.027 --> 00:00:09.360
Common examples include developer tools, code repositories, documentation lookup,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.176
file and knowledge-base access, ticketing systems, calendars and email at a high level,

00:00:14.176 --> 00:00:17.880
databases, cloud services, internal APIs, monitoring systems, business workflow automation,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.361
and local productivity tools.

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:23.436
These use cases are broad, but they share a common need:

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:27.140
the AI system must connect to something outside the model.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.362
Developer workflows are a natural fit.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:32.696
An assistant may read project documentation, inspect repository metadata,

00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:36.030
summarize issue history, or help draft a change request.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.882
Operations workflows are another fit.

00:00:37.882 --> 00:00:41.956
An assistant may retrieve monitoring context, summarize alerts, look up runbooks,

00:00:41.956 --> 00:00:44.920
or create a ticket for a human team.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.994
Business workflows may use approved access to calendars, documents, customer records,

00:00:48.994 --> 00:00:52.698
or internal knowledge bases to help users complete routine tasks.

00:00:52.698 --> 00:00:54.921
Some use cases should be read-only.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.143
Documentation lookup, status retrieval, schema inspection,

00:00:57.143 --> 00:01:00.847
and search can often provide value without changing external systems.

00:01:00.847 --> 00:01:04.181
Other use cases are write-capable and require stronger controls.

00:01:04.181 --> 00:01:07.144
Creating tickets, sending messages, modifying files, updating records,

00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:11.589
changing cloud resources, or triggering workflows can affect real people and systems.

00:01:11.589 --> 00:01:16.034
The difference between reading and acting should be obvious to the user

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:17.886
and enforced by the application.

00:01:17.886 --> 00:01:22.331
The best use cases start with a clear job to be done.

00:01:22.331 --> 00:01:27.147
What does the user need, what data is required, what action is allowed,

00:01:27.147 --> 00:01:31.221
which identity should be used, and what evidence should be logged?

00:01:31.221 --> 00:01:32.332
Without that clarity,

00:01:32.332 --> 00:01:38.259
teams can end up connecting tools because they are available rather than because they are appropriate.

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:42.333
MCP adoption works best when capabilities are tied to well-bounded workflows,

00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:48.260
not treated as a universal permission to let the model do anything connected systems can do.
